      <problem>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>If $$F(x) = f(x)g(x)$$ , where $$f$$ and $$g$$ have derivatives of all orders, show that $$F^{''} = f^{''}g + 2f^{\prime}g^{\prime} + fg^{''}$$ .</item>
          <item>Find similar formulas for $$F^{'''}$$ and $$F^{(4)}$$ .</item>
          <item>Guess a formula for $$F^{(n)}$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="65" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Find expressions for the first five derivatives of $$f(x) = x^{2}e^{ x}$$ . Do you see a pattern in these expressions? Guess a formula for $$f^{(n)}(x)$$ and prove it using mathematical induction.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>$$f^{\prime}{(x)} = {({x^{2} + 2x})}e^{ x}$$ , $$f^{''}{(x)} = {({x^{2} + 4x + 2})}e^{ x}$$ , $$f^{'''}{(x)} = {({x^{2} + 6x + 6})}e^{ x}$$ , $$f^{(4)}{(x)} = {({x^{2} + 8x + 12})}e^{ x}$$ , $$f^{(5)}{(x)} = {({x^{2} + 10x + 20})}e^{ x}$$ ; $$f^{(n)}{(x)} = \left\lbrack {x^{2} + 2nx + n{({n - 1})}} \right\rbrack e^{ x}$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="66" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>Reciprocal Rule If $$g$$ is differentiable, the Reciprocal Rule says that</paragraph>
        <paragraph math="block">$$\frac{d}{\mathit{dx}}\left\lbrack \frac{1}{g(x)} \right\rbrack = - \frac{g^{\prime}(x)}{\left\lbrack {g(x)} \right\rbrack^{2}}$$</paragraph>
        <list type="ordered">
          <item>Use the Quotient Rule to prove the Reciprocal Rule.</item>
          <item>Use the Reciprocal Rule to differentiate the function in Exercise 14.</item>
          <item>Use the Reciprocal Rule to verify that the Power Rule is valid for negative integers, that is, $$\frac{d}{\mathit{dx}}\left( x^{- n} \right) = - nx^{- n - 1}$$ for all positive integers $$n$$ .</item>
        </list>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>
